\end{table}

Several key observations emerge from these results:

\textbf{Scattering features drive generalization performance.} All model configurations achieve AUC in the range 0.81--0.85, including the flat SVM baseline (AUC: 0.834). This consistency strongly suggests that the wavelet scattering features themselves---rather than the downstream architecture---are the primary factor enabling cross-patient discrimination. The parameter-free, deformation-stable nature of scattering coefficients makes them inherently robust to inter-subject variability.

\textbf{Temporal modeling improves over flat classification.} All graph-temporal models improve F1-score over the SVM baseline (0.471), with GCN+BiGRU achieving the highest F1 of 0.547 (+16.1\% relative improvement). This confirms that modeling the temporal evolution of brain state representations adds discriminative value beyond static feature classification.

\textbf{Sensitivity-specificity tradeoff varies by architecture.} The GAT-based models achieve higher specificity (0.842--0.844) but lower sensitivity compared to GCN+BiGRU, suggesting that attention-weighted graph processing produces more conservative predictions. The GCN+BiGRU model achieves the best balance with sensitivity of 0.634 and specificity of 0.837.

