% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Feature Engineering vs.\ Model Complexity}

Our results reveal an important insight for the seizure prediction community: \textit{the quality of input features matters more than architectural complexity for cross-patient generalization}. The wavelet scattering transform achieves AUC of 0.834 even with a simple SVM classifier, and adding sophisticated graph-temporal architectures improves F1-score but not AUC substantially. This finding aligns with the theoretical properties of scattering---its Lipschitz continuity to deformations provides a mathematical guarantee of robustness to the inter-subject signal variations that plague learned feature extractors.

\subsection{PLV Graphs and Attention}

Contrary to our hypothesis, GAT with PLV-based graphs did not consistently outperform GCN with fully connected graphs. We attribute this to several factors: (1) the PLV threshold ($\tau = 0.3$) may be suboptimal, potentially excluding informative weak connections; (2) with only 17 channels, the graph is small enough that GCN's isotropic message passing is sufficient; and (3) PLV estimation from 5-second windows may lack statistical reliability. Future work should explore adaptive threshold selection and larger electrode montages.

\subsection{Temporal Architecture Comparison}

The BiGRU temporal encoder outperformed both the Transformer and Mamba SSM in our experiments. The Transformer's quadratic complexity provides no advantage at sequence length 8, while its lack of inductive temporal bias may require more data to learn effective temporal patterns. The Mamba SSM, implemented in pure PyTorch without optimized CUDA kernels, may suffer from numerical precision issues in its sequential scan computation. With proper CUDA-accelerated implementations and longer sequences, Mamba's linear complexity and selective memory mechanism would likely demonstrate greater advantages.

\subsection{Clinical Implications}

The achieved sensitivity of 0.634 with specificity of 0.837 represents a meaningful step toward clinical utility for cross-patient models. The high AUC (0.848) suggests that with optimized decision thresholds---potentially personalized per-patient using brief calibration recordings---the sensitivity-specificity tradeoff could be substantially improved. The use of parameter-free scattering features also facilitates edge deployment, as feature extraction requires no GPU and can be computed in real time on embedded hardware.

\subsection{Limitations}

Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, our evaluation uses only 3 patients from the CHB-MIT dataset; a comprehensive evaluation on 15+ patients is necessary for robust conclusions. Second, the CHB-MIT dataset contains pediatric patients, and generalization to adult epilepsy populations requires validation on datasets such as the Siena Scalp EEG Database or the TUH Seizure Corpus. Third, our data collection protocol included predominantly seizure-containing recording files, creating artificially balanced class distributions that may not reflect clinical reality. Fourth, the cuDNN incompatibility on our compute infrastructure necessitated disabling GPU-accelerated RNN operations, potentially affecting temporal model performance.


% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
% ============================================================

We presented a modular graph-temporal framework for cross-patient EEG seizure prediction, demonstrating that wavelet scattering features provide robust, parameter-free representations that generalize effectively across patients. Our systematic ablation across spatial encoders (GCN, GATv2) and temporal encoders (BiGRU, Transformer, Mamba SSM) reveals that feature quality---not architectural complexity---is the primary determinant of cross-patient performance, with all configurations achieving AUC of 0.81--0.85. The best configuration (GCN+BiGRU) achieves F1 of 0.547 with sensitivity of 0.634 and specificity of 0.837 under rigorous LOSO evaluation.

Future work will pursue several directions: (1) scaling evaluation to 15+ patients with complete recording files; (2) incorporating adversarial domain adaptation via gradient reversal layers to explicitly enforce patient-invariant feature learning; (3) validating on additional datasets including adult epilepsy cohorts; (4) exploring longer temporal sequences (60--120 seconds) to better leverage Mamba's selective memory capabilities; and (5) optimizing for real-time deployment on wearable EEG hardware with latency and power constraints.


